  "notes": "A literary verb from {案|あん} (idea, thought). Has a variant form {案|あん}ずる (classical conjugation). The proverb {案|あん}ずるより{産|う}むが{易|やす}し (giving birth is easier than worrying about it — i.e., things turn out easier than you fear) is well known.\n\nUSAGE:\nSense 1 (worry) is more common and carries a literary, somewhat elevated tone compared to {心配|しんぱい}する. Sense 2 (devise) is related to the noun {案|あん} (idea, plan) and appears in formal or written contexts.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {身|み}を{案|あん}じる — to worry about someone's safety\n- {将来|しょうらい}を{案|あん}じる — to worry about the future\n- {行|ゆ}く{末|すえ}を{案|あん}じる — to worry about what lies ahead\n- {策|さく}を{案|あん}じる — to devise a strategy\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {心配|しんぱい}する — to worry; the most common everyday word for worrying\n- {気|き}にする — to be concerned; more casual, about minor worries\n- {懸念|けねん}する — to have concerns; formal, used in official or written contexts",
